      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "No, no, no, my friend, I'm afraid I got a bit carried away with the goblin analogy. Think of it like this: when your lawn is struggling, it's like a group of goblins are trying to sabotage its growth. But, I digress.\n\nTo address your question, when it comes to phosphorus deficiency, you'll want to look for a fertilizer with a higher phosphorus content. A good rule of thumb is to look for a product with a P2O5 ratio of at least 10-10-10 or 10-20-10. This will provide your lawn with the necessary phosphorus to promote root growth and green color.\n\nHowever, if you suspect a phosphorus deficiency, I'd recommend a product with a higher phosphorus content, such as 10-20-10 or 10-30-10. These products will provide a more significant boost to your lawn's phosphorus levels.\n\nKeep in mind that it's also possible that your lawn is experiencing a nitrogen deficiency, which can cause yellowing or stunted growth. In this case, you may want to look for a fertilizer with a higher nitrogen content, such as 20-5-5 or 15-5",
